-- title:
--   Distinctness of the two branch points ξ_∞ ≠ ξ₀
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$ but $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ whose image in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$, and with $M/p$ nonzero; assume the $q$-expansion `jqModC` of $j$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC` generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the integral form ratios at level $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, so that the two-chart integral model of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p = \mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ is available, and let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a term of `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, i.e. a package recording that this model is proper, flat, integral, locally of finite presentation and normal over $R_p$, that the auxiliary level-$\Gamma_N$ model is proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$, together with a curve model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the function field of $X_H(M)$ identified with the geometric generic fibre compatibly with the Galois action and with the chart coordinates, and the remaining data of the Deligne–Rapoport description of the fibre at $p$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $A$, whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$, and let $\rho \colon R_p \to A$ be a ring homomorphism whose composite with the inclusion $A \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the structure map. Let $O$ be a local commutative ring whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of $p$, with a ring homomorphism $\rho_O \colon R_p \to O$, and let $O \to \kappa_A$ be a ring homomorphism whose composite with $\rho_O$ is $\rho$ followed by the residue map of $A$. Then the two points $\xi_\infty$ and $\xi_0$ of the base change of the model along $\rho_O$ that $\mathfrak{X}$ attaches to this data — the images of the generic points of the two branches of the geometric special fibre at $A$ — are distinct.
--
--   In the Deligne–Rapoport description of $X_H(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$, the special fibre is a union of two branches crossing at the supersingular points; this statement records that the two branch generic points remain distinct after base change to $O$, so that the crossing configuration is genuinely reducible. It is the starting point for the analysis of the crossing point, being used in the comparison of the branch ideals at a crossing point and in the local reading of the chart there.
-- source:
